/***************************************************************************************************
* 🔥 LEETCODE 100 — Same Tree (Recursive O(N) Solution)
* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 🧩 PROBLEM:
* Given the roots of two binary trees `p` and `q`, determine if they are *identical*.
*
* Two binary trees are considered the same if:
*   → They have the same structure.
*   → Corresponding nodes have the same value.
*
* Example:
* Input:  p = [1,2,3], q = [1,2,3]
* Output: true
*
* Input:  p = [1,2], q = [1,null,2]
* Output: false
*
* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 💡 INTUITION:
* This is a **classic recursive tree problem**.
* - We traverse both trees **simultaneously**.
* - At every step:
*     → If both nodes are NULL → they match (true).
*     → If one is NULL and other isn’t → they differ (false).
*     → Otherwise, check:
*         (a) Current node values are equal.
*         (b) Left subtrees are identical.
*         (c) Right subtrees are identical.
*
* If all three conditions hold true for all nodes → trees are same.
***************************************************************************************************/

// ✅ CLEAN CODE — O(N) TIME COMPLEXITY
class Solution {
public:
    bool isSameTree(TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
        // Base case: if either node is null
        if (p == NULL || q == NULL)
            return p == q;  // true only if both are NULL

        // Check left and right subtrees recursively
        bool leftSame = isSameTree(p->left, q->left);
        bool rightSame = isSameTree(p->right, q->right);

        // Both subtrees must be same and current node values must match
        return leftSame && rightSame && (p->val == q->val);
    }
};

/***************************************************************************************************
* 🧠 STEP-BY-STEP ALGORITHM:
* 1️⃣ If both `p` and `q` are NULL → return true.
* 2️⃣ If only one of them is NULL → return false.
* 3️⃣ If both are non-NULL:
*       a) Compare node values → if unequal, return false.
*       b) Recursively check left subtrees.
*       c) Recursively check right subtrees.
* 4️⃣ Return true only if:
*       → Current node values match.
*       → Left subtrees are same.
*       → Right subtrees are same.
***************************************************************************************************
* 🧩 DRY RUN EXAMPLE:
* Input: p = [1,2,3], q = [1,2,3]
*
*           1           1
*          / \         / \
*         2   3       2   3
*
* ➤ Compare root(1,1) → equal
* ➤ Compare left(2,2) → equal
* ➤ Compare right(3,3) → equal
* ✅ All matched → return true
***************************************************************************************************
* ⚙️ EDGE CASES:
* 1️⃣ Both trees empty → true
* 2️⃣ One tree empty → false
* 3️⃣ Different structure:
*     p = [1,2], q = [1,null,2] → false
* 4️⃣ Same structure, different values:
*     p = [1,2,3], q = [1,2,4] → false
* 5️⃣ Single node trees with same value → true
***************************************************************************************************
* ⏱️ TIME COMPLEXITY:  O(N)
* Each node is visited once.
*
* 💾 SPACE COMPLEXITY: O(H)
* Recursion stack space = height of tree (O(log N) for balanced, O(N) for skewed).
***************************************************************************************************
